----------------------------------

Success! Glorious, triumphant victory! Well, not that glorious (9 wishes 
and several blessed genocides), but still pretty triumphant, and 
certainly victorious.

"So now you're cured?" my friends and coworkers ask me.

"Heck, no!" I reply, "You don't stop writing just because you get 
published and win an award. You don't stop climbing once you've peaked 
your first mountain. This is only the beginning." There is a wild smile 
on my face and a manic gleam in my eye. Visions dance before me of yet 
more ascensions--ascensions in other classes, stylish ascensions, 
restricted ascensions.

I have to stop, control my breathing, compose myself.

And this message.

----------------------------------

I /was/ working on the story of Kwirq the chaotic male elven mage, but :

1. It was taking far too long, and this post is already days late. 
Besides, there's nethack to be played!
2. I'm not such a gifted storyteller that you wouldn't all have been 
bored to tears, that tried to read it. Alas, it's true.
3. Most embarrassingly, I can't remember all the details. This /might/ 
have something to do with the staying-up ALL night to play. And I 
(lazily, foolishly) never take notes. Really, I ought to compile one of 
those game recorder patches or whatever...

I played this game from Thursday mid-afternoon until Saturday (very) 
early morning, in around 30 hours and 67066 (should have waited ONE more 
turn before offering the amulet!) turns. I played through ssh windows to 
my own compiled version on my openbsd server, with the colour hp monitor 
patch. I made a lot of mistakes, and took a few cheap shots (genocide, 
wishes, polypiling like crazy), but overall I'm really quite pleased 
with my progress over the last year.

Actually, I'm amazed at how there've been very discrete jumps in my 
ability to progress a character. Once upon a time I would quaff from 
every fountain until I either died or got a wish, or would re-roll 
players until I get some sweet starting kit, but now I'm a bit 
embarrassed by that behaviour, and proud that I can take any starting 
character and put up at least a good show. (Barring, you know, the 
occasional falling rock trap in the first room ;-). There was a time 
when I was lucky to get to the mines. Then lucky to get to the oracle. 
Lucky to see the Big Room. And then, in a short space of time: quest, 
castle, ascension.

Special thanks to...
- All the spoiler authors, for helping me have half a clue what I was doing,
- Dylan O'Donnell, for the Gazetteer (and everything else), my road map,
- Kate Nepveu and Eva Myers, for helping me find those spoilers,
- The great folks of RGRN, and Google, for answering my questions before 
I asked them, and
- The DevTeam, for thinking of everything.

(Also, my agent, my publicist, blah, blah, blah. ;-)

ANYWAY, ANYWAY, ANYWAY, and please, I apologize for being so long-winded 
and dull (exuberant and self-indulgent feeling because of my first 
ascension, really!), here're the details of the journey:

* Kwirq had nothing exceptional in his starting inventory, and decent 
int+cha stats
   (I notice that these actually last longer than the ones with a better 
start)

* I found a (promptly converted) altar somewhere around Dlvl 7 and sac'd 
until I got Magicbane

* The next level down had a white unicorn, and though I coveted the 
horn, I (wisely) ran away

* ... to the Mines! The minetown altar was crossaligned. Izchak had a 
magic lamp for me, though ^_^

* A bones level a little deeper had (all cursed) a BoH, another magic 
lamp, and "oReflection

* Back to the fountains and altar of the main dungeon to mix up some 
holy water!

* Deeper in the mines, a gnome or something jumped on a poly trap and 
became an arch-lich. Run away!

* The Oracle was on Dlvl 9, and Dlvl 10 was the Big Room. Liberal use of 
the E-word saves me.

* Since I already had 2 BoH's, of course I get another one in Sokoban.
   (this is hilarious, since before then, for months and months, I 
_only_ ever got reflection.)

* At some point I blessed geno all L and go get my luckstones. All h, too.

* Use one of the magic lamps for SDSM. Find speed boots sometime around 
here.

* By the time I reach the quest, I'm eligible, and The Dark One is 
laughable. Tengu's give tele and control.

* Medusa's Island is the version with the halls on opposite sides of the 
level. I have to jump-spell my way across (and believe me, that gets old 
fast!)

* /oCold the moat of the Castle, zap down the door, and bash the monsters.

* I wish for blessed charging, and eventually SDSM again (!!) after 
accidentally over enchanting the first

* I've been carrying a tinning kit in my BoH expressly for dealing with 
trolls. The castle has about a dozen tins of troll meat kicking around.

* Go looking for the portal to, find, and clean out Fort Ludios

* Donate the rest of that gold to the minetown priest but don't get 
appreciable increase in protection.

* Cheese off Anhur somehow and lose all my protection. Eventually get 
_some_ of it with the VotD priest.
   (Upon rumination I see I _should_ have gone back and taken out that 
Minetown priest. You know, being chaotic and all)

* Since finding +poly, I've been piling _everything_ until I've pulled 
out everything that was good or "shuddering vibrations"ed it all away. I 
lose about three dozen scrolls into a paper golem (lesson!). I also 
blank my ?charging when I go for a swim in the moat to dilute potions.

* Since full healing is smoky, alchemy gives me more HP _and_ a few 
wishes. After two tries I get Frost Brand (woohoo!)

* Exploring Gehennom is pretty neat. I'm playing at my friends' house, 
on their big TV, and they don't want to watch me go through all the 
nooks and crannies, so we use lots of +dig and ^T (wizards rock!). We 
pay off Asmodeus, but then he disappears (and we were so hoping to kill 
him and take the money back--alas!).

* Orcus town is spooky and cool. Orcus summons Yeenoghu, and we 
hurriedly dispatch them, fearing Demogorgon. All Yeenoghu manages to 
summon though are two succubi, whom we promptly call Kandi and Sherri 
and have a few good turns with.
(Avoiding from my demonic playmates is easy since eating a quantum 
mechanic a while back. Running away after an encounter to redone my 
armour, I--tragically, tragically!--make the comment to my friends 
"well, I _am_ intrinsically fast.")

* I've started to realize the folly of polypiling spell books. Or at 
least, spell books that you might _need_ later. Well, at least I now 
have lots of blank spell books, in case I ever get a magic marker. At 
this point I've used up all my wishes, on things like gauntlets of dex 
and helm of brilliance--naturally I find both these items after not too 
long. The b+FingerOfDeath was a good choice, though. (I never get 
crowned. Just not lucky enough, I guess.)

* Vlad is a wimp. Later on, I come back to his tower and cursed geno 
nurses, but foolishly leave the central area and get pinned in the 
corridor while the rest of them eventually teleport away. net benefit: 
about 50 HP.

* Map out the rest of Gehennom (the hard, greedy way--also, magic 
mapping is one of my forgotten spells).

* Eventually get a magic marker, write charging, write some spell books 
(force bolt, magic missile, extra healing, cure sickness, dig), go back 
to the bottom and find the vibrating square.

* Take out Rodney (death ray). Go run down and perform the Ritual of 
Invocation. Very neat. Enter the sanctum, Rodney already has the amulet. 
Kill, take. Run up through Gehennom (very glad I dug straight paths).

* Stupidity: obsess now over lack of ?gold detection for the planes, run 
back through the dungeons and the mines, polypiling every tool hoping 
for a magic marker. fail. Kill Rodney a few more times, tin him for good 
luck.

* Enter the plane of Earth. Magic portal is in the third cavern I try.

* Enter the plane of Air. Use up most of my /Death killing air 
elementals, since "oYendor is such a big Pw hit. Wander around the right 
side of the map until I hit the portal.

* Fire plane is annoying for the fifty bajillion "The fire elemental 
steps on a fire trap! The fire elemental is not hurt!"  messages. 
Nicely, the portal is on the same side of the screen and plainly 
(planely) visible.

* Water plane is very cool. Also empty of all ; (blessed geno), which 
means pretty darn empty!

* The Astral Plane is very, very busy, and kinda scary. Mostly just 
annoying, though. I try the right altar first, but it's lawful. 
Pestilence makes me glad to have cure sickness at 0%. Next I try the 
middle chamber (cram rations, yay), and am successful.

* Feelings of vast, almost overpowering relief.

* In my haste to see my final stats, I do forget to save the map of the 
Astral plane, though. What a shame to lose such an important souvenir. :-(

Good God, that was long, even summarized. Again I apologize. ^_^;;

